[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65990"] :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-66008"] :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{1}{4}$ is in the range of the function." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-70788/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "ec6fb2f4-6409-5f8c-9de1-0877bd94f296" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70788/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b6531706-52db-5400-949f-ecbd6d0fe366" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70788/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1bf70dbf-b85e-5c28-b7f4-a3efc0ca6799" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70788/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "37b87e1c-1246-5a69-bfa1-1fabe99c00fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70788/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c31f4cff-8e11-5baa-abb3-fcd85423ab1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70788/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fada6eb1-77b5-567e-be9e-3c7e7ee41126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-70788